>
  <p>
    Most fake chat generators give you a conversation and stop there. The details that make a
    <strong>fake WhatsApp chat screenshot</strong> look genuine — a timestamp that isn't a round
    number, a reaction on the right message, a missed call just before the apology — are things
    you add afterwards, by clicking directly on the phone preview.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide covers every field you can edit and every element you can add: message text,
    timestamps, reactions, photos, voice notes, call logs, replies, avatars and the chat header.
    It's free, there's no signup and no watermark. Open the <a href="/editor">free chat
    editor</a> in another tab and follow along.
  </p>

  <h2>Two ways to edit a fake chat</h2>
  <p>Everything in the preview works one of two ways:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Click text and type.</strong> If it's text on screen, it's editable in place.</li>
    <li><strong>Hover a message for the buttons.</strong> Two round buttons appear next to the
      bubble — one for reactions, one for a menu of things to add.</li>
  </ul>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/editor-hover-actions.webp" alt="Hovering a fake WhatsApp message bubble reveals a reaction button and a three-dot edit menu" loading="lazy" width="798" height="159" />
    <figcaption>Hover any message and two buttons appear: reactions (left) and the message menu (right).</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>How to edit the message text</h2>
  <p>
    Click into a bubble and it behaves like a text box — type, select, paste, delete. The bubble
    resizes as you go, so you can see immediately whether a line wraps awkwardly on a phone
    screen.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/editor-inline-text.webp" alt="Editing the text of a fake WhatsApp chat bubble directly in the live preview" loading="lazy" width="618" height="147" />
    <figcaption>Message text is edited in place — click, select, retype.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>How to change the timestamp on each message</h2>
  <p>
    Every message carries its own timestamp, and every one is editable. Click it, type whatever
    you want — <code>9:41 am</code>, <code>23:04</code>, <code>Yesterday</code> — then press
    <strong>Enter</strong> to save or <strong>Esc</strong> to cancel.
  </p>
  <p>
    This matters more than it sounds. Generated times are evenly spaced three minutes apart,
    which no real conversation ever is. Breaking that rhythm — two messages in the same minute,
    then a forty-minute gap — is the single cheapest way to make a fake chat screenshot look
    real.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/editor-timestamp.webp" alt="Changing the timestamp on a fake WhatsApp message by clicking it in the preview" loading="lazy" width="618" height="138" />
    <figcaption>Timestamps are individually editable. Enter saves, Esc cancels.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>How to change the contact name and status</h2>
  <p>
    The header title and the line under it are both editable. The status line is whatever the
    platform would show — <code>online</code>, <code>last seen recently</code>,
    <code>Active now</code>, or a member count in a group chat — and you can replace it with
    anything. Click the avatar beside it to upload your own picture.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/editor-header.webp" alt="Editing the contact name, status line and profile picture in a fake WhatsApp chat header" loading="lazy" width="540" height="162" />
    <figcaption>Name, status line and profile picture — all editable from the header.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The grey pills in the feed are editable too: date separators like <code>Monday</code> and
    group events like <code>Aisha added Lina</code>. Click and retype.
  </p>

  <h2>How to add a reaction to a message</h2>
  <p>
    Hover a message and click the smiley. Nine quick reactions appear — ❤️ 😂 😮 😢 👍 👎 🔥 😍 👏 —
    and clicking one attaches it to that message. To remove a reaction, click the badge itself.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/editor-reaction-picker.webp" alt="The emoji reaction picker open above a fake WhatsApp chat bubble with nine reaction options" loading="lazy" width="753" height="234" />
    <figcaption>Nine one-click emoji reactions — click the badge again to remove one.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Use them sparingly. Two or three reactions across a conversation reads as real; a reaction on
    every message reads as a demo.
  </p>

  <h2>How to add photos, voice notes, calls and replies</h2>
  <p>
    The three-dot button is where the rest of the chat lives. Every item inserts directly
    <strong>below</strong> the message you opened it on, so you're always placing things exactly
    where the story needs them rather than appending to the end.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/editor-message-menu.webp" alt="The edit menu on a fake chat message showing options to add a message, reply, image, date, voice note, voice call and missed call" loading="lazy" width="324" height="455" />
    <figcaption>The full message menu. In a group chat, an extra "Add event below" item appears.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Add message below</strong> — a new empty bubble. Click it and type.</li>
    <li><strong>Reply to this</strong> — a new message with the quoted-reply block attached,
      like tapping reply in the real app.</li>
    <li><strong>Add image below</strong> — opens a file picker and inserts your photo as an
      image message.</li>
    <li><strong>Add date below</strong> — a date chip in numeric form, e.g. <code>3/8/2026</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Add day below</strong> — a day chip instead, e.g. <code>Monday</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Add voice note below</strong> — a voice message with a real waveform and an
      editable duration.</li>
    <li><strong>Add voice call below</strong> — a completed call log entry.</li>
    <li><strong>Add missed call below</strong> — the red missed-call entry.</li>
    <li><strong>Add event below</strong> — group events like "X added Y". Group chats only.</li>
    <li><strong>Delete</strong> — removes that message.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Here's what a voice note and a missed call look like once they're in the feed:</p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/editor-added-items.webp" alt="A fake WhatsApp chat with a missed voice call entry and a voice note with a waveform added" loading="lazy" width="540" height="450" />
    <figcaption>A missed call and a voice note — with editable durations and timestamps.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>How to change avatars and profile pictures</h2>
  <p>
    Click any profile picture in the feed to upload an image for that person. Until you do,
    everyone gets a coloured circle with their initials, and each participant is assigned a
    distinct colour — so a four-person group chat never ends up with two identical avatars.
  </p>
  <p>
    In a group chat, clicking the avatar in the header sets the <em>group</em> photo instead of
    an individual's.
  </p>

  <h2>How to preview the chat as an animated video</h2>
  <p>
    Above the phone there's a small playback bar. <strong>Play</strong> runs the conversation the
    way the exported video will — messages appearing one at a time with typing indicators, the
    feed scrolling as it fills. The speaker button mutes the message sounds, and the speed button
    cycles through 1x, 1.5x, 2x and 0.75x.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/editor-playback.webp" alt="The chat preview playback bar with play, mute and speed controls" loading="lazy" width="623" height="90" />
    <figcaption>Play, mute and speed — the speed setting also applies to the exported video.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    That speed setting isn't just for the preview: it changes the pacing of the exported MP4 too.
    If your <a href="/chat-story-video-maker">chat story video</a> feels slow on TikTok, bump it
    to 1.5x here before exporting.
  </p>

  <h2>How to switch platform, theme and device</h2>
  <p>
    The right-hand panel restyles the whole conversation without touching your text:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Platform</strong> — the same conversation rendered as
      <a href="/fake-whatsapp-chat-generator">WhatsApp</a>,
      <a href="/fake-instagram-dm-generator">Instagram</a>,
      <a href="/fake-messenger-chat-generator">Messenger</a>,
      <a href="/fake-telegram-chat-generator">Telegram</a>,
      <a href="/fake-slack-conversation-generator">Slack</a>,
      <a href="/fake-discord-chat-generator">Discord</a>,
      <a href="/fake-chatgpt-conversation-generator">ChatGPT</a>,
      <a href="/fake-claude-conversation-generator">Claude</a> or
      <a href="/fake-gemini-chat-generator">Gemini</a>. One click swaps the entire look.</li>
    <li><strong>Theme</strong> — light or dark mode.</li>
    <li><strong>Device</strong> — an iOS status bar with a notch, or Android with a punch-hole.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your work saves itself</h2>
  <p>
    Edits are saved to your browser automatically, so closing the tab by accident doesn't cost
    you the conversation. Nothing is uploaded — the chat, the avatars and the images all stay on
    your device, which is also why there's no account to make.
  </p>

  <h2>The fastest order to do it in</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Write or paste the script, pick your character, hit <strong>Generate Chat</strong>.</li>
    <li>Fix the timestamps first — it's the detail that gives away a generated chat.</li>
    <li>Add reactions to the two or three messages that deserve them, not every message.</li>
    <li>Insert the extras — a photo, a voice note, a missed call — where the story needs them.</li>
    <li>Upload avatars, then set the chat name and status line.</li>
    <li>Press <strong>Play</strong> and watch it once. Awkward pacing is obvious in motion and
      invisible in a still.</li>
    <li>Export a PNG screenshot, or an MP4 for a chat story video.</li>
  </ol>

  <figure>
    <img class="phone-shot" src="/blog/editor-phone.webp" alt="A finished fake WhatsApp chat screenshot with reactions, read receipts and edited timestamps" loading="lazy" width="540" height="1170" />
    <figcaption>The finished result — every element here was edited directly in the preview.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <h3>How do I change the time on a fake WhatsApp chat?</h3>
  <p>
    Click the timestamp under any message in the preview and type a new one — <code>9:41 am</code>,
    <code>23:04</code> or even "Yesterday". Press Enter to save or Esc to cancel. Every message
    has its own timestamp, so you can vary the gaps between them.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I add reactions to a fake chat?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. Hover a message and click the smiley button to open the reaction picker, then pick one of
    nine emoji — heart, laughing, wow, sad, thumbs up, thumbs down, fire, heart eyes or clapping.
    Click the reaction badge again to remove it.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I add voice notes, photos and missed calls?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. Hover a message and open the three-dot menu. It can insert a photo, a voice note with a
    waveform, a completed call log, a missed call, a quoted reply, a date chip or another message —
    each placed directly below the message you opened the menu on.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I reorder messages in the editor?</h3>
  <p>
    There's no drag-and-drop yet. Every menu item inserts below the message you opened it on, so
    add each new message in the right place to begin with. For a large reshuffle it's faster to
    edit the script and generate the chat again.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I edit a fake chat on my phone?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. The editor works on mobile, where the script and preview become tabs you switch between.
    Tap-to-edit works the same as click-to-edit, and the hover buttons appear on tap.
  </p>
  <h3>Do my edits show up in the exported image and video?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. Everything visible in the preview is exported in both PNG and MP4, including reactions,
    voice notes, call logs and edited timestamps. There's no watermark on either.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ethics">Use it responsibly</h2>
  <p>
    Fake chat tools are for <strong>clearly fictional</strong> content: stories, skits, memes,
    product demos, presentations and teaching examples. Don't use them to impersonate real
    people, fabricate evidence, or mislead anyone — that can be illegal, and it's why the export
    asks you to confirm your chat is fictional. If you're publishing somewhere it could be taken
    for a real conversation, label it as a mockup.
  </p>

